Texture parking holds each LZ4 copy in host memory until its repack, so the park file gets the copies that stay
(Minato, patch 0024).

Why: 0021 wrote every park's LZ4 copy into the park file at once, and a second later the LZFSE repack wrote the copy
that stays: about 70 % of the bytes written were LZ4 copies that lived about a second, and a texture deleted or used
again within that second was written for nothing. One load of a visual novel on the iPhone wrote ~8 GB of flash,
almost all of it textures the game parked and deleted unused. Now the pack keeps its LZ4 copy in host memory while
the held copies stay under 128 MiB and the process is neither in the emergency band nor in a memory warning's second;
the repack writes only the LZFSE copy, reading the LZ4 copy from memory. A repack that saves nothing writes the LZ4
copy as it is. In the emergency band or a warning's second nothing is held and the held copies go to the file at once
(a plain write, up to 4 at a time); past the cap a pack writes as before. MINATO_ANGLE_PARK_HOLD=0 turns it off.
ANGLEMetalParkingLine appends " hold=<MiB> hpeak=<MiB>".
Test: graphics/tests/angle_texture_parking.mm --store (fwrite at most the file's bytes + 25 %: 1676 -> 479 MiB for
465 MiB; hpeak at most 128 MiB; the footprint and bit-for-bit checks unchanged).
Upstream: no (Minato's memory policy).

